{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If his offering is an olah.</b> It is difficult: why did it change from the style of the two sections arranged after this — ״וְאִם מִן הַצֹּאן וְגוֹ׳ לְעוֹלָה״, ״וְאִם מִן הָעוֹף עוֹלָה״ — for here too it should have said ״אִם מִן הַבָּקָר עוֹלָה״ וְגוֹ׳? And it may be explained according to the reasoning that they stated in Torat Kohanim (3:20) regarding what the verse says above this, ״אֶת קָרְבַּנְכֶם״ — this teaches that it may come as a voluntary offering of the community; thus far. Therefore it says, immediately after saying ״קָרְבַּנְכֶם״, <b>if an olah</b> — meaning, specifically an olah is what I permit to come as a voluntary offering of the community, and not shelamim, as it is taught (Torat Kohanim 3): shelamim do not come as a voluntary offering of the community." "Further, if the verse had said ״אִם מִן הַבָּקָר עוֹלָה״, it would have been understood that it permitted bringing an olah only from the cattle and not from the flock. Therefore it said <b>if an olah</b>, to say that what it multiplies in saying ״קָרְבַּנְכֶם״ is a specification of the olah, whether it be from the cattle or whether it be from the flock. And one should not err regarding the olah of the bird, for the verse already clarified and said ״קָרְבָּנוֹ״ in excess, as they expounded in Torat Kohanim, to exclude the community from the olah of the bird." "And in Torat Kohanim (3:21) they said, and this is their language: ״עוֹלָה״ — what does this come to teach? For one might think that all these disqualifications apply only to a voluntary olah; from where do we learn an obligatory olah? etc. Scripture comes to teach ״אִם עֹלָה״; thus far. It appears that the difficulty they raised was that it should have said ״אִם קָרְבָּנוֹ עוֹלָה״, and they expounded that if it had said ״אִם קָרְבָּנוֹ עוֹלָה״, then it would have been understood that the verse is speaking specifically of a voluntary olah, which is the subject of the verse, as it is written ״כִּי יַקְרִיב״ — voluntary, and it says ״אִם עֹלָה״ — meaning every olah in the world, whether obligatory or whether voluntary. And the Rabad, may his memory be for blessing, explained that the word ״עוֹלָה״ is superfluous, that it could have relied on its mention at the end, but this is a stretch:" "<b>His offering.</b> In Torat Kohanim they expounded that it comes to include shelamim that are subject to all the listed disqualifications, and one should not say that the verse comes to exclude them, saying ״קָרְבָּנוֹ״ as stated — which is an olah and not shelamim — for this does not require exclusion, for from where would one derive a prohibition? Nor did they expound ״קָרְבָּנוֹ״ in the suffixed form, which is more explicit in the verse, for they already derived this law from ״קָרְבָּנוֹ״ stated regarding the olah of the flock (Leviticus 1:10)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:52:38.541996737Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}